5. YOUR DATA
   The Software coordinates native coding agents, such as Claude Code or Codex,
   that you install and sign in to yourself. It keeps its task graph, events,
   messages and the text of the prompts it sends in a Neo4j database that you
   provide and control, whether your own or a local one started from the compose
   file distributed with the Software. It keeps credentials, briefs and logs in
   private files on your machine. The native agents keep their own transcripts,
   and send what they are given and the work they do to the model provider you
   signed in to, under your own agreement with that provider; the Software itself
   sends nothing to any model provider. Deleting a swarm removes its database
   records, not those files or transcripts. While a prompt is delivered to an
   agent its text is briefly visible in your machine's process list, so do not
   put secrets in prompts or messages.

   The copyright holder does not collect, transmit, or receive your data, and the
   Software's own code contains no analytics or telemetry that reports off your
   machine. Third-party programs it starts or works with, including the native
   agents, have their own behaviour and settings; the compose file distributed
   with the Software turns off Neo4j's anonymous usage reporting.

   Agents, and any hooks you configure, act with your own permissions. The
   Software does not sandbox them or separate them from each other. You are
   responsible for what they do, and for the security of your own database,
   credentials, native agent logins, and any third-party model provider you use.

